<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-21054] build: mpitests configure check does not link, broken MPI fails build</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21054</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;LB_CONFIG_MPITESTS in config/lustre-core.m4 checks whether the MPI&lt;br/&gt;
tests can be built with AC_COMPILE_IFELSE, so the test program is&lt;br/&gt;
only compiled ("mpicc -c") and never linked. A broken MPI library&lt;br/&gt;
installation passes the check, mpitests are enabled, and the build&lt;br/&gt;
then fails later when linking the programs in lustre/tests/mpi&lt;br/&gt;
(createmany_mpi, cascading_rw, mdsrate, ...).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;--enable-mpitests is enabled by default and uses whatever "mpicc"&lt;br/&gt;
is in PATH, so this hits builds that did not ask for MPI tests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Seen on ARM64 Ubuntu clients with OpenMPI 5.0.11 installed&lt;br/&gt;
system-wide for Slurm. That libmpi.so was built on a host with&lt;br/&gt;
Lustre headers, so it has a NEEDED entry for liblustreapi.so.1.&lt;br/&gt;
When building Lustre on a client where Lustre is not yet installed,&lt;br/&gt;
liblustreapi.so.1 cannot be found and the link in lustre/tests/mpi&lt;br/&gt;
fails with:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  error while loading shared libraries: liblustreapi.so.1: cannot&lt;br/&gt;
  open shared object file&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Steps to reproduce:&lt;br/&gt;
1. Put an mpicc in PATH whose libmpi.so cannot be linked (e.g. has&lt;br/&gt;
   an unresolvable NEEDED library).&lt;br/&gt;
2. ./configure (without --disable-mpitests)&lt;br/&gt;
   -&amp;gt; "checking whether mpitests can be built... yes"&lt;br/&gt;
3. make (or make debs)&lt;br/&gt;
   -&amp;gt; link failure in lustre/tests/mpi&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Workaround: configure with --disable-mpitests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fix: use AC_LINK_IFELSE in the mpitests check so that an MPI&lt;br/&gt;
library that cannot be linked disables mpitests at configure time&lt;br/&gt;
instead of failing the build.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
